+---
+description: "Completed assistant visualizations with native iframe isolation, readable fallback text, related-question lists, and additive response-presentation guidance."
+kind: "package-reference"
+---
+
+# @deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-intelligent-ui
+
+English | [中文](README.zh.md)
+
+## Summary
+
+This optional Web plugin renders assistant `openui` fences inline with the answer, in the chat's own font and colors, using OpenIntelligentUI's sandboxed rendering approach and design system plus a small component kit and icon set. It adds component, picture, citation, and follow-up question guidance to model requests, and keeps fallback explanations and original source available when a visualization cannot run. Assistant `followups` fences render as a Related list whose rows ask the next question.
+
+## Table of Contents
+
+- [Use this package](#use-this-package)
+- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)
+- [Dev Note](#dev-note)
+- [Model Experience](#model-experience)
+- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)
+
+<a id="use-this-package"></a>
+## Use this package
+
+The Web bundle mounts this plugin beside chat. It contributes two entries at priority `0` to the chat-owned `conversation.chat.message.code` renderer chain: one accepts only `openui` language fences, the other only `followups` fences. The chat's default code renderer handles other languages. User messages remain code. Set the Host config `enabled` to `false` to disable additive guidance; this switch does not hide previously generated visualizations or related-question lists, or replace model/persona settings.
+
+A fence body is either an HTML fragment or JSON. A body starting with `{` or `[` is JSON; any other body is a fragment and must contain at least one tag. In a fragment, the first `<title>` element names the frame and the loading placeholder (otherwise a localized default), each inline `<script>` element runs in its original order after the remaining markup is inserted, and `<style>` elements stay in the markup. The surrounding answer carries the explanation, so a fragment has no fallback field. JSON uses `version: 1`, nonempty `title`, nonempty `fallback`, and `html` as a string or string array; optional fields are `initialHeight`, `css`, `jsFunctions`, and `jsExpressions` (string or string array), and unknown fields are rejected. The source limit is 128,000 characters; titles are limited to 200, JSON fallback to 16,000, JSON CSS/functions to 32,000 each, and JSON fragment arrays or fragment scripts to 128 entries. Heights stay between 24 and 2,400 CSS pixels; taller content scrolls inside the frame. Measuring the content root keeps expansion and shrinkage independent of the embedding viewport. A remounted block starts at its last measured height. A block executes as soon as its fence closes, while the rest of the answer may still stream; until then it shows a quiet placeholder with its title and a generating status. Stopped, unclosed, invalid, or oversized blocks retain original code with a localized status.
+
+The frame has no border or title bar and a transparent background, so a component reads as part of the answer; a source button stays visible on devices without hover, and appears on desktop when the pointer hovers over the component or keyboard focus reaches the button. Before mounting, the page reads the chat's font family and size, text, muted, line, link, and state colors, and background; each value passes an allowlist and falls back to fixed palette values. The frame design system defines those as `--fg`, `--muted`, `--subtle`, `--line`, `--accent`, `--fill`, `--bg`, and OpenIntelligentUI's `--color-text-primary`-style names, styles headings, links, code, tables, and form controls, and provides OpenIntelligentUI's SVG color classes (`c-blue`, `c-teal`, and others, with text classes `t`, `ts`, `th`). The component kit adds `ui-card`, `ui-grid`/`ui-item`, `ui-list`, `ui-tags`/`ui-tag`, `ui-flow` with `ui-node`, `ui-arrow`, `ui-group`, `ui-cols`, and `ui-bar`, `ui-steps`, `ui-stats`/`ui-stat`, `ui-profile`/`ui-media`, `ui-callout`, and the tone modifiers `success`, `warning`, `danger`, and `info`. An element with `data-icon="name"` receives the matching icon from 235 bundled Lucide icons and 29 aliases, including elements that scripts add later; only icons whose names appear in the source are sent to the frame. An HTTPS link request displays its complete address and Open link / Cancel controls outside the frame. Only clicking the host-owned Open link button opens a new tab without opener access. Further frame messages cannot replace the displayed address while confirmation is pending; cancellation, failure, or retry clears it. JSON fallback text appears after a failure. A script error keeps the frame and whatever it rendered and adds a localized notice. Retry rebuilds only local frames without a model request. Remote pictures stay outside the sandbox in the existing Markdown image renderer.
+
+A `followups` fence body holds one question per line. The renderer strips bullets, numbering, and wrapping quotation marks, and drops blank lines, duplicates, lines over 200 characters, and every question after the fifth. While the fence is open, or when no question remains, nothing renders. A closed fence becomes a localized Related heading above full-width rows divided by hairlines, each row a native button with a trailing chevron, so Enter and Space activate it. The section is omitted when the Session cannot take a prompt: the `session/prompt` access answer is denied, the Session was removed, or it belongs to a subagent that is not continuable or whose parent is unavailable.
+
+A click goes through the Session's composer input actions. With an empty composer (plain mode, blank draft, no attachments), the question replaces the blank draft and is submitted, so it becomes the next user message and queues behind a running turn like a typed one. During adjudication or submission, the rows are disabled. When the composer holds text, attachments, or a claimed command, the click only inserts the question at the caret; it never discards the draft, and the input actions offer no way to focus the composer afterward. If the editor rejects an insertion, a localized retry notice appears and the draft stays unchanged. A row stays inert after its question was sent, so a double click cannot send it twice; the other rows remain available.
+
+<a id="understand-the-implementation"></a>
+## Understand the implementation
+
+A trusted outer frame and a generated inner frame each use `sandbox="allow-scripts"`, opaque origins, and denied browser permissions. Both documents put a fixed content-security policy before authored data: no network, external scripts, imports, evaluation, fonts, media, nested frames, workers, objects, forms, or base URLs. Inline scripts/styles and data images are allowed. The outer `frame-src 'none'` also contains external self-navigation by the inner frame. Authored metadata, base elements, scripts, stylesheet links, frames, and objects are stripped before HTML insertion. JavaScript fields run only as inline script text in the inner frame.
+
+The outer frame sends one fixed start signal after its initial load; authored content is inserted only after that signal. It accepts only source- and token-matched ready/error/finite-height/link messages from its inner frame and forwards those fixed states; a link message is forwarded only for an HTTP(S) address of at most 2,048 characters, and the page parses it again before displaying a confirmation request. A matching token identifies a frame, not a trusted user action; even synthetic clicks and forged messages from that frame cannot navigate the host without a separate click on its confirmation control. An address with a user name or password is not offered. The request scrolls into view and is announced politely; its open button becomes available 500 ms after the request appears, and the component ignores further requests for one second after the reader opens or cancels, so a double click cannot land on a destination the reader never saw. The embedding iframe carries the frame's color scheme so the browser keeps its canvas transparent. The page accepts them only from its exact outer frame. A script error is reported once and leaves the frame mounted. A subsequent inner load reports failure instead of leaving a blank component. There is no arbitrary-code message, host RPC, prompt-sending, credentials, file, or parent-DOM bridge. Height reports are bounded. Unmount removes the listener, timeout, and both frames. Initialization has a 15-second deadline; a missed deadline or a later inner load removes the affected frame and retains text/source. Inserting the inner document uses a replacer function, so `$$`, `$&`, and `$'` in authored code stay literal.
+
+The related-question list is ordinary React content outside these frames. It renders each model-written line as text and has no script, frame, or host bridge; its only effect is a Session input action after a user click.
+
+This is a DSH adaptation of [OpenIntelligentUI](https://github.com/CopilotKit/OpenIntelligentUI)'s content fields and isolated presentation method. It is not an AG-UI transport or the upstream application runtime. Native nested frames replace the upstream generic host RPC bridge. [Third-party notices](THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md) retain its MIT license; [upstream.json](upstream.json) records the exact reviewed source.
+
+Package tests cover HTML fragment parsing, JSON limits, host style allowlisting, icon selection, safe serialization, literal `$` sequences, untrusted link requests and host confirmation, streaming/stopped/unclosed rejection, independent instances, cleanup, forged messages, bounded resize with content shrinkage, source preservation, retry, scoped prompt assembly, related-question parsing, the section's streaming, access, and click behavior, and chain registration. The keyless Web test at [intelligent-ui.e2e.ts](../../../apps/web/tests/intelligent-ui.e2e.ts) covers live controls, containment failures, blocked network/navigation, confirmation of synthetic and forged link requests, scrollable long content with repeated expansion/collapse, and 320/390/1280-pixel geometry.
+
+<a id="dev-note"></a>
+## Dev Note
+
+Keep generated content inside the nested frame isolation and route external links through host confirmation. Validate changes with the package tests and the keyless Web scenarios linked above, including touch access to source and narrow-screen containment.
+
+<a id="model-experience"></a>
+## Model Experience
+
+### Response presentation guidance
+
+#### What the model sees
+
+The independent `deployment:response-presentation` section encourages useful interactive explanations and actual tool-returned pictures, defines numeric Markdown citations (numbers written side by side, as in `[1][3]`, merge into one chip), asks for one to three inline components in substantive answers, and asks those answers to end with a `followups` fence of two or three short questions in the user's language. It lists the component kit, common icon names, the SVG color classes, and the color variables, shows two short examples (an icon card grid and a flow diagram), states the isolation restrictions and a size budget, and tells the model to write each block directly in the answer, without reading DSH source code or creating files, builds, previews, screenshots, or tests for it. Its full text lives in [presentation-prompt.ts](src/presentation-prompt.ts). Scoped empty persona suffixes do not suppress this named section; disabling `enabled` omits it.
+
+#### Token effect
+
+The enabled section adds its fixed guidance tokens to every assembled model request in this Web composition. Generated `openui` source and `followups` fences also consume response tokens and subsequent conversation-context tokens. The renderer and local retry make no model calls and add no further request tokens; clicking a related question submits an ordinary user message, which makes its own request.
+
+#### KV Cache effect
+
+The section stays stable while its text and composition remain unchanged. Enabling, disabling, or editing it changes the prompt prefix from its insertion position onward; reuse of earlier cached content depends on the provider and matching request prefix. No cache-hit improvement is claimed.
+
+## Known Limitations and Deferred Work
+
+<a id="known-limitations-and-deferred-work"></a>
+
+- Generated scripts can change or blank their own content, and heavy synchronous JavaScript can stall its renderer. The sandbox supplies no CPU budget or guarantee of factual accuracy; essential conclusions remain in fallback prose.
+- Components cannot load libraries, fonts, or remote images; pictures inside a component must be inline SVG or `data:` URLs. A component appears when its fence closes rather than growing element by element while it streams.
+- Chromium has keyless isolation and navigation-containment coverage. Safari and Firefox remain unverified. This plugin provides no AG-UI streaming compatibility or image-generation service.
+- Related questions use the composer. A click on a composer that holds a draft inserts the question without sending it. The Markdown renderer appends the source list after the last block, so a `followups` fence at the end of an answer appears above it.
